???: For the longest time, I never really had a home. 

???: When I was a puppy, I was abandoned by my old owners.

???: I never really understood why. I still don’t.

???: But in the end, it was out of my control.

???: …

???: I was abandoned. A stray, lost and forced to wander the streets.

???: A few things rang loudly in my head.

???: Thirst. Hunger. Desperation…

???: … Fear.  

???: …

???: I didn’t want to give up. But the world around me wished I did.

???: My legs were tired. My will to keep walking was waning.

???: Until even my very heart couldn’t take it anymore.

???: …

???: Just as I was about to lie down and accept my fate, I smelled a fragrant scent.

???: It felt invigorating. I knew I’d die with a smile if I were to die at that very moment. 

???: I had already accepted my fate. But suddenly… I wished I could live for just a little bit longer.

???: I wanted to find where this scent came from. It’s the least I could do to say thank you.

???: And so… I searched for that scent. 

???: However, my body was weak. With every second it only got weaker.

???: I didn’t know how much time I had left, but I kept searching nonetheless.

???: …

???: My search led me to a hole in a garden fence.

???: I didn’t care if it was dangerous, I wanted to see the source of that lovely scent. 

???: Without a second thought, I entered. And past the garden fence was…

???: A paradise. A lovely garden filled with flowers and animals.

???: There were birds, cats, squirrels… And so many more.

???: Cautiously I walked in. But thankfully, nobody seemed to mind my entry.

???: If anything, it looked like they were welcoming me.

???: Everyone was peaceful, living in harmony.

???: … I wondered if I was still even alive. Maybe I died a long time ago, before I even smelled that scent.

???: …


???: But I can’t stop here. I’m this close to finally seeing it.

???: And so I went further into the garden. My nose helped me for one last time to follow that scent, getting closer to the source.

???: …

???: And finally, I found it. 

???: Within this whole garden… A single, pink flower.

???: Trying to say thank you, I barked a few times at it. 

???: But of course, flowers don’t speak. They don’t have to.

???: I smelled its scent for one last time, the fragrance gracing me in a warm embrace.

???: And so… I laid down next to the flower.

???: It was finally time for me to go, after all. 

???: I was afraid, but…

???: Perhaps, if I were to die here right beside this flower… It’ll be alright.

???: I smile with that thought. 

???: …

???: What was left of my little strength finally fades away, and my eyes slowly close.


???: …

???: But… For some reason, my fate didn’t end there.

???: My eyes opened. I was in shock – how was I still alive?

???: I scaDiwaed my surroundings. It looked like I was in someone’s living room. 

???: And… In front of me were two bowls. One with food, one with water.

???: Instinctively, I ate the food and water in a blink of an eye. 

???: I wasn’t hungry, thirsty, cold, nor desperate anymore… I couldn’t help but cry softly.

???: …

Elderly Man: “Ah, you’re awake. Boy, I was worried I was too late.”

???: I was startled by the man. At the time, I was still a bit scared, so I couldn’t help but back off and bark once.

Elderly Man: “Easy now. I’m not here to hurt you.”

???: Of course, he meant no harm. He’s who took me into his home, after all.

???: I was cautious but… Soon enough, I slowly approached the man who saved me.

???: …

???: I stood there in front of him, unsure what to do. He patted me on the head soon after.

Elderly Man: Ah… What's your name?

???: ?

Elderly Man: Haha right. You can't talk.

Elderly Man: You know, you gave me quite the scare there. I thought you were gone.

Elderly Man: But look at you, so bright and full of vigor. What spirit!

Elderly Man: For that, I think I'll call you Diwa. 

Diwa: …

Diwa: Bark!

Elderly Man: Seems like you like it!

Diwa: I did. I did like it.

Diwa: The human inspected my dirty broken body.

Elderly Man: I’m guessing you were abandoned?

Diwa: I didn’t know how to respond back then, so I turned my head away and whispered softly instead.

Elderly Man: Oh… Well, if you’d like, you could stay here with me and the others.

Diwa: A new home? I looked at the man’s face, he seemed nice enough. 

Diwa: But would abandon me too?

Diwa: …

Diwa: Yet, his kind smile and honest eyes, blocked only by thick transparent lenses, convinced me I can trust him.

Diwa: And so, I barked happily at the idea of being taken in.

Elderly Man: Alright then. Welcome to our family, “Diwa”. 

Diwa: I jumped to his embrace as I heard those words. He hugged me warmly in response.

Elderly Man: Haha! Come here!

Diwa: At last… I found my new home.

Diwa: …

Diwa: As the years passed by, I became a loyal dog to Gramps.

Diwa: I would watch him water and take care of his lovely garden. Even after all these years I still can’t believe how wondrous of a place it is.

Diwa: Sometimes, I played with the other animals in the garden. We’d run around and frolic in both the sun and rain.

Diwa: Sometimes Gramps would join us too, though he can’t play so much because of his old age.

Diwa: Without realizing it, I had grown up.

Diwa: …

Diwa: Sometimes, Gramps would leave the house and garden for a few days. Rarely, those days would turn to a whole week without him.

Diwa: Whenever he was gone, I would stay inside the house and take care of myself. 

Diwa: I’m glad he taught me how to take care of myself.

Diwa: …

Diwa: Gramps would always come back though, thankfully. Usually with a toy or a nice treat. 

Diwa: … Sometimes even cute dog clothes that I would rarely wear. 

Diwa: But for once… One of his trips would take longer than usual.

Diwa: At first, I thought it was a week-long trip out. But then came another week without Gramps.

Diwa: …

Diwa: And then, a third week without Gramps.

Diwa: … Where did Gramps go?

Diwa: I was told to never leave the house except if I had to tend to the garden but… I had to know. 

Diwa: And so I left, looking for Gramps.

Diwa: Thankfully, I was smarter this time, so I could survive living on the streets. 

Diwa: Though it was as cold and scary as I had remembered it to be, I didn’t stop in my search.

Diwa: …

Diwa: My search took days. Those days turned to weeks, and weeks turned to months.

Diwa: Eventually...

Diwa: I gave up. 

Diwa: I was never going to find him like this.

Diwa: As I prepared to head back home, some part of me was wishing that maybe...

Diwa: Just maybe, he might be there waiting for me.

Diwa: And if not… The garden and friends I have back home can cheer me up. 

Diwa: I told them I'd be back. I miss them dearly.

Diwa: I remembered the way back home, thankfully, and arrived after traveling for a few days.

Diwa: …

Diwa: I was gone for too long.

Diwa: The garden was in disarray. The trees stood still, but the flowers and other plants were left dry.

Diwa: My friends and the other animals were gone. They must’ve left now that the garden was in this state.

Diwa: Will they come back?

Diwa: I'm here! Friends! Please don't leave me alone...

Diwa: ...

Diwa: I was stupid. 

Diwa: I was so focused on trying to find Gramps that… I forgot about my duty: To take care of the garden – of the pride and paradise he created for strays like me.

Diwa: …

Diwa: But, I had to move on. I went back inside the house, passing by the dying garden.

Diwa: Yet… Gramps still wasn’t there. Where was he?

Diwa: I was terrified again. My new home didn't feel like home anymore. It was gone.

Diwa: And its owner, Gramps… Gone as well. 

Diwa: The reality of it all was setting in when…

Diwa: A familiar scent graced my nose once more.

Diwa: I knew where that scent came from this time, even if it was so faint that even my nose could barely pick it up.

Diwa: I rushed over to my beloved pink flower.

Diwa: …

Diwa: There it was. It was on the brink of dying, but… Still, it hung on.

Diwa: Were you waiting... for me?

Diwa: The flower didn't speak as usual.

Diwa: I’ve gotta do something… What did Gramps always do in times like this?

Diwa: Ah! I know what the flower needs. 

Diwa: Water. It needs water, fast.

